From ad1b97c7cfb18cce7ce38b12b63fbe7c04057325 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-v4 <v4@localhost>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:47:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] v4: byte-offset IV strength reduction for reduction loops
 + fix latent dot-product IV bug
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reduction loops (F64/F32/I32 sum and dot, AVX2 and SSE2) now step a
byte-offset induction variable (stride = elem_size * vec_width) instead of an
element index, mirroring the matmul path:
- the GEP offset becomes the byte IV itself (per-iteration shl/leaq/scale gone),
- VecLoad takes (base, byte_iv) SIB addressing directly,
- the loop bound becomes floor(N/vec_width)*byte_stride (running ceil
  iterations would read/write past the array end — the OOB class already fixed
  in the matmul path),
- the scalar remainder start becomes byte_iv_final/elem_size.

Falls back to the element-index scheme when the offset chain is not the
canonical shl/mul(iv_cast, elem_size) shape.

This exposed and fixed a latent bug: the dot-product transform removed two
instructions at accumulator_add_idx+4, which only happened to delete dead
scalar code while the element-index Step 2 kept inserting per-GEP multiplies
that shifted indices. With byte-offset IV the same removal deleted the loop's
induction-variable increment (infinite loop / undefined IV). It now removes
only the dead scalar add and lets DCE reclaim the dead multiply.

Validation: 5 reduction kernels x N in 0..300 x {AVX2,SSE2} bit-exact vs
scalar reference and GCC; large-N (1M) exact; regression 126/126, correctness
50/50, intrinsics 3/3. Benchmarks unchanged-to-slightly-better (F64 sum/dot at
GCC parity, F32 dot 2x GCC).
